\section{Channel values}{

A handful of tokens describe a \emph{channel value} -- something bound to
data, computed, or otherwise resolved per row -- rather than one fixed R
type. Most mark encodings (\verb{x =}, \verb{y =}, \verb{fill =}, ...) accept all of
these; see \code{vignette("getting-started")} for worked examples of each.

\describe{
\item{\code{~column}}{A one-sided formula naming a data column, e.g.
\code{x = ~mpg}. Its values go through the channel's scale. A formula is
always a column, even when the name is also a color or symbol name:
\code{symbol = ~square} uses a column named \code{square}.}
\item{\code{~constant}}{A one-sided formula holding a single string,
number or \code{TRUE}/\code{FALSE}, e.g. \code{fill = ~"medium"} or \code{r = ~5}: a
constant that \emph{does} go through the scale, so it gets the same color
(or radius, symbol, ...) the scale gives \code{"medium"} (or 5) in the data
-- e.g., to label a group in its own color. Compare \code{r = 5}, a radius
of 5 pixels. (Sent to Mosaic as a SQL literal, e.g.
\code{sql("'medium'")}.)}
\item{\code{constant}}{A constant value applied to every row, e.g.
\code{fill = "steelblue"}, \code{symbol = "square"} or \code{opacity = 0.5}. It is
used as is, not passed through the scale. (A string that isn't a
valid constant for the channel, e.g. \code{fill = "species"}, is still
read by Mosaic as a column name, for now; write columns as formulas.)}
\item{\code{transform()}}{A vgplotr transform function, e.g. \code{vg_bin()},
\code{vg_count()}, \code{vg_avg()} -- computed by DuckDB, not R. See
\link{vg_transforms} for the full set.}
\item{\code{sql()}/\code{agg()}}{A raw SQL expression (\code{sql()}) or one wrapping
an aggregate function (\code{agg()}), for anything a column reference or
transform can't express. See \code{\link[=sql]{sql()}}.}
\item{\code{list(value=, ...)}}{The \verb{\{value:, scale:, label:\}} object form
mosaic-spec's schema allows for overriding a channel's scale/label
directly, e.g. \code{x = list(value = ~mpg, scale = "shared")}. Mosaic
0.31.0 doesn't handle this form when rendering yet (the plot fails
with a SQL error; see
\url{https://github.com/uwdata/mosaic/issues/1311}), so avoid it for
now.}
}

A property whose notation includes \code{list(value=, ...)} (not just
\code{list}) is one of these \verb{\{value:, scale:, label:\}} channels; a bare
\code{list} elsewhere means an ordinary named list of sub-options specific to
that property (its own \verb{@param} text says what goes in it).
}

\section{Reactive values}{

\describe{
\item{\code{param()}}{A reactive Param or Selection, e.g.
\code{param(brush)} -- see \code{\link[=param]{param()}}. Appears on almost every argument:
mosaic-spec lets nearly any scalar option be driven by a live
\code{param()} instead of a fixed value (a slider-controlled radius, a
menu-controlled color scheme, ...), not just data-bound channels.}
}
}

\section{Plain R types}{

\describe{
\item{\code{number}}{A single R number, e.g. \code{2}, \code{0.5}.}
\item{\code{string}}{A single character string, e.g. \code{"steelblue"}.}
\item{\code{boolean}}{\code{TRUE} or \code{FALSE}.}
\item{\code{NULL}}{Literal \code{NULL} -- explicitly unsets/disables the
property (e.g. \code{label = NULL} shows no axis label), distinct from
just leaving the argument unset.}
\item{numeric vector / character vector / vector}{A plain R vector,
e.g. \code{range = c(0, 20)}; \code{vector} when mosaic accepts elements of
more than one type (or doesn't constrain them further).}
\item{\code{list}}{A named list of sub-options specific to that property,
e.g. \code{margins = list(left = 5, top = 5, right = 5, bottom = 5)} in
\code{\link[=vg_plot_defaults]{vg_plot_defaults()}}.}
\item{\code{any}}{Genuinely unconstrained by mosaic's own schema -- e.g. a
\code{\link[=vg_menu]{vg_menu()}}'s initial \code{value}, which can be whatever type its
\code{options} are.}
}
}

\section{Fixed sets of literal values}{

Quoted options (\code{"basis" | "bundle" | ...}) are the literal strings (or,
occasionally, \code{TRUE}/\code{FALSE}/\code{NULL} mixed into the same set) mosaic
actually recognizes there -- pass exactly one of them, quoted, e.g.
\code{curve = "basis"}. A set with more than 10 values is abbreviated to the
first 10 plus \code{...}; see the property's own description (same line) or
\href{https://idl.uw.edu/mosaic/}{mosaic's documentation} for the rest --
most commonly \href{https://observablehq.com/plot/features/scales#color-scales}{Observable Plot's color schemes} for
a \verb{scheme =} argument.

One special literal, \code{"Fixed"}, appears on domain-like arguments
(\verb{x_domain =}, \verb{color_domain =}, ...): it freezes a domain that was
initially computed from the data so it no longer changes under
subsequent interactive filtering, for stable before/after comparisons.

Two more appear together, \code{"day"/"week"/"month"/...} and \code{number}, on
interval-like arguments (\verb{interval =}, \verb{ticks =}, ...): either a named
time interval (\code{"day"}, \code{"week"}, \code{"3 months"}, ...) or a plain number,
defining intervals at that many underlying units.
